  "dtype": "int",
  "citation": "McCullagh, P. (1980), Regression Models for Ordinal Data, Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B 42(2), 109-142, DOI 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1980.tb01109.x. Table 1, p. 111, `Tonsil size of carriers and non-carriers of Streptococcus pyogenes`, reads carriers 19, 29 and 24 over the three ordered sizes against non-carriers 497, 560 and 269, totalling 72, 1326 and 1398. The original data are Holmes, M. C. and Williams, R. E. O. (1954), which the paper cites for them.",
  "notes": "THE ROW MARGIN OF THE PUBLISHED 2 x 3 TABLE, EXPANDED TO ONE ROW PER CHILD, in the order the table reads: size 1 first, its 19 carriers then its 497 non-carriers, then size 2, then size 3. The three ordered sizes are coded 1 `present but not enlarged`, 2 `enlarged` and 3 `greatly enlarged`, which is the order the page prints them in and the only thing about them this model uses.\n\nWHY IT IS CODED AND NOT LABELLED. A dataset declares ONE dtype, and a column of the three phrases would build as a plain object Series -- which carries no declared order, and which the node this dataset is written for refuses for exactly that reason. Integers in the published order say the same thing in a form the loader can build.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT SUPPORT. The 2 x 3 table is the whole of the published data, so nothing here identifies a child, an age or a year, and the order within a cell is this transcription's rather than the source's. It carries no oracle case: McCullagh prints his estimates to three decimals, which no tolerance class in tests/oracle/_policy.json can express, so the module's own tests assert them with round() instead."
